99589	99739	99586	99587	the Baron	2298	“ although I grant you that the principal advantages of travel must be the opportunity which it affords us of becoming acquainted with human nature , knowledge , of course , chiefly gained where human beings most congregate , great cities , and , as you say , the Courts of Princes ; still , one of its great benefits is , that it enlarges a man ’s experiences , not only of his fellow - creatures in particular , but of nature in general . Many men pass through life without seeing a sunrise : a traveller can not . If human experience be gained by seeing men in their undress , not only when they are conscious of the presence of others , natural experience is only to be acquired by studying nature at all periods , not merely when man is busy and the beasts asleep . ”
